<% content_for :title, "Demos · yrby" %>

<section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
  <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl font-normal tracking-tight text-zinc-100 sm:text-5xl">Demos</h1>
  <p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-zinc-400">
    Each demo binds a different Yjs shape to the same Rails backend. Five of
    them share one <code>DocumentChannel</code>, which stores every update with
    <code>Y::Document</code> and relays it to the room. That channel has no
    code specific to any demo. The Lexxy demo uses a <code>NoteChannel</code>
    built from lexxy-realtime's install generator, and it also renders the
    document to HTML on the server after each change. Every demo page has a
    panel that reads the stored document back in Ruby.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-zinc-400">
    New to yrby? Start with the <a class="text-accent underline" href="/examples/document">record-backed editor</a>.
    It runs the quickstart code on a single record, with
    <code>collaborative_document_tag</code> and a CodeMirror editor.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 rounded-lg border border-amber-500/25 bg-amber-500/8 px-4 py-3 text-sm leading-6 text-amber-200/90">
    Each demo opens in a new room with its own URL. Share that URL to edit
    together. Rooms are public, so anyone with the link can edit, and a room is
    deleted after <%= Limits::ROOM_IDLE_TTL / 3600 %> hours with no edits and no
    one connected. Please don't paste anything private.
  </p>

  <ul class="demo-list">
    <% @demos.each do |demo| %>
      <li>
        <%# nofollow: each launch mints a fresh, per-visitor room, not a page
            for a crawler to follow (robots.txt disallows the room paths too). %>
        <%= link_to demo.title, demo_path(demo.slug), rel: "nofollow" %>
        <code><%= demo.shape %></code>
        <span><%= demo.blurb %></span>
      </li>
    <% end %>
  </ul>
</section>
